# issue #149: `InverseFunctionalObjectProperty(ObjectInverseOf(:p))` IS
# `FunctionalObjectProperty(:p)` — the characteristics SWAP across polarity. So
# with C and D disjoint, `A ≡ ∃p.C ⊓ ∃p.D` forces its two p-successors to merge
# and clash: A is UNSATISFIABLE.
#
# rustdl reports A satisfiable with an EMPTY `dropped` — the axiom is silently
# ignored (both #144-era consumers guard on `!role.is_inverse()`, and unlike
# symmetry these must NOT be relaxed the same way; see #149 for why the naive
# fix was measured and rejected). The same miss is pinned engine-side by
# `symmetric_inverse_polarity::inverse_functional_on_an_inverse_role_is_still_missed`.
#
# This fixture replaced `chaincompose` as the acceptance set's detection when
# the #108 certificate fix made `classify_internal` agree with chaincompose's
# oracle and tripped `the_detection_set_has_not_silently_gone_vacuous` — the
# THIRD time that guard has done its job (chainrange -> chaincompose -> this).
#
# The claim rests on hand-derivation; the polarity identity is textbook
# (Functional(p) ≡ InverseFunctional(p⁻)). HermiT and Konclude both accept the
# construct (recorded in inverse_polarity_guards.rs); neither has been run on
# THIS file yet — pair any peer probe with a discriminating control first, per
# the chaincompose oracle's Konclude caveat.
provenance: derivation-only   # Functional(p) ≡ InverseFunctional(p⁻); see #149
unsatisfiable: A
